nstitutions including alliances violet  universal and the lazarus the  congregation of the mission of friends  but something important is about the  foreign after archives in Iran in Tehran  it is very important because at the time  there was a special department in the  Ministry of Foreign Affairs which was  exclusively responsible for non Muslim  cases ok adoree Mlle Mouton ave which is  literally the department of diverse  people but in fact it was the department  of non-muslims so there I could find  lots of collections of non-muslim cases  individuals and groups which illustrate  the Iranian Society with regard to non  Muslim population it's very important  that I very good very good so tell us a  bit about the four groups you look at  you look at the National local  international level so talk about their  daily lives a little bit tell us about  them yes if you want to illustrate a  kind of daily life daily life of these  minorities the Austrians Christian Jews  and bahais one could see that they had  their own quarters separated from the  majority Muslim majority these quarter's  could be inside or outside the cities  and could have walls or gates or could  not have them but the goal was to  isolate the pop  relation and there were some regulations  and codes for them when they were coming  outside of their quarters but these  rules were very arbitrary and local so  sometimes for example you see that they  were not allowed to have the markets or  shops inside the city sometimes they had  to follow some dress codes for example  sometimes especially for the Austrians  and Jews they had two very patcher kind  of they call it vastly a kind of badge  to be recognized in the society and  sometimes they were not allowed to come  outside when it was raining because  non-muslims were considered impure or  nyjah's so the Tau test during the rain  this impurity with wash off Wow and  sometimes they were not allowed to ride  a horse or bicycle and that was a sign  of inferiority or humiliation and they  could be distinguished because of their  you know dress codes and appearance and  sometimes because of their language or  accent and sometimes because they were  following the rituals of their religions  but the most similar community to  Muslims were the Bobby Baha'i community  as far as dress codes or language we're  concerned they were so similar so they  could not be so easily distinguished in  the community because the Bob ISM  appeared in 1844 in Iran and obviously  the first followers were the converts  from other major religions and Muslims  in particular but something important is  that despite all these distinctions and  minority religious minorities would  communicate and interact with the  majority they would trade they would  marry each other and oh they would marry  yeah they would marry that there were  some rules of course but  that would happen and they recalled a  very communication with each other in  the daily life and it's important to  know that when we are reading documents  about the time normally we are reading  something exceptional because normally  we write reports and something unusual  happens something something abnormal or  occasional happens so it's important to  know that when we are reading the  documents we should not generalize it  through the whole society for all the  time so if we bear this in mind we  should see that we can see that there  were not very rigid borders and the  borders were flexible and sometimes  regulations would be postponed and even  ignored because of economic or political  reasons very interesting you also talked  about the constitutional revolution what  was the role of these religious  communities in the revolution yes the  constitutional revolution happened in  1906 in Iran and it's very controversial  because their research about this event  is very varied and their interpretations  are very conflicting but for me it's  important to focus on the goals of the  color of